===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act by which something burns or is burned.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1828|author=Timothy Flint|journal=The Western Monthly Review|volume=1|page=403|passage=It gives a fine delineation of the '''burnings''' of shame, disappointed ambition, and vengeance{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1850|magazine=The Edinburgh Review, Or Critical Journal|volume=91|page=93
|passage=The propriety of the dissolution, too, was speedily seen in the improved state of the public peace: for twelve years we hear little of Orange riots, and nothing of such '''burnings''' and wreckings as those of Maghera, Maghery, and Annahagh.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Paul P. Murphy and Samira Said|title=Louisiana arson suspect expressed disgust with Baptist churches on Facebook|url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/04/12/us/louisiana-church-fires-suspect|date=2019-04-12|passage=The suspect is the 21-year-old son of a St. Landry Parish sheriff’s deputy and may have been influenced by “black metal” music and its “associated history with church '''burnings''',” the state fire marshal’s office said.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260102103906/https://www.cnn.com/2019/04/12/us/louisiana-church-fires-suspect|archivedate=2 January 2026}}
# A [[fire]].
#: {{ux|en|The '''burnings''' continued all day.}}
# {{lb|en|cryptocurrency}} The purposeful removal of certain number of coins in circulation, by sending them to a public address where the private keys cannot be obtained (called burn address, eater address or black hole), which usually should be available on the [[blockchain]] for anyone to review such a transaction. It’s a one-way address with no ability to reverse the transaction or withdraw the coins. For all practical purposes, the asset no longer exists (it has been “burned”). The act of burning effectively removes tokens from the available supply.
# A fiery pain.

